---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Treadmill Speed Chart: mph to Pace and km/h, 2 to 12 mph";
const description =
  "Treadmill speed chart: 6 mph = 10:00/mile, 7.5 mph = 8:00 and 10 mph = 6:00 (16.1 km/h). Every speed from 2 to 12 mph in pace per mile and km/h, plus incline.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Pace & Speed Chart", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I convert treadmill mph to minutes per mile?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Divide 60 by the speed in mph. At 6 mph that is a 10-minute mile; at 4 mph, a 15-minute mile. Those two are worth memorising because everything else can be estimated from them. To get km/h, multiply mph by 1.61 — or just 1.6, which is accurate enough at any treadmill speed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What speed is a 10-minute mile on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "6.0 mph, which is 9.7 km/h. A 9-minute mile is about 6.7 mph, an 8-minute mile is 7.5 mph, and a 12-minute mile is 5.0 mph. If your target pace falls between the speeds your machine offers, round down rather than up.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What incline equals running on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly, walking at 3 mph on a 10 to 12% grade is comparable in energy cost to jogging at about 5 mph on the flat (around 7.4 to 8.3 METs against 8.7 by the ACSM equations), at a fraction of the impact per footfall. These are approximations that vary with body weight and fitness, and they assume you are not holding the handrails.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I set the treadmill to 1% incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is a harmless default and a reasonable habit, particularly if you race outdoors. The 1996 study behind it found the energy cost matched outdoor running at 1% specifically at faster paces, around 7 mph and up. At walking and easy jogging paces the difference is small enough to ignore.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my treadmill pace different from my outdoor pace?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Several reasons unrelated to fitness: no air resistance to push through, no camber or terrain variation, and poorer heat dissipation indoors, which makes a given pace feel harder. Console calibration can also drift with belt wear. Treat treadmill pace as its own scale rather than expecting the two to match.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What treadmill speed do I need for a 30-minute 5K?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 30-minute 5K is a 9:39 mile, which is 6.21 mph or a 6:00 kilometre. Treadmills step in 0.1 mph, and 6.2 mph finishes in about 30:04, so set 6.3 mph to get under 30 minutes. A 25-minute 5K needs 7.5 mph and a 20-minute 5K needs 9.4 mph.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good treadmill speed for walking and for running?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most people walk at 2.5 to 4 mph, with 3 to 3.5 mph a typical brisk pace (a 20:00 to 17:09 mile). Running usually starts around 4.5 to 5 mph, and most recreational runners do their easy miles between 5 and 6.5 mph. Faster than about 8.5 mph is interval and sprint territory for most people.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-09-08",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "best-of");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-08",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill pace chart",
      "treadmill speed conversion",
      "mph to minutes per mile",
      "treadmill km/h",
      "running pace",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Chart", link: "#the-chart" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/best-of/">Best Lists</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Reference</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Speed Chart: mph to Pace and km/h</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Pace per mile is 60 ÷ mph: 6 mph is a 10:00 mile, 7.5 mph is 8:00 and 10 mph is 6:00. The chart below converts every treadmill speed from 2 to 12 mph to minutes per mile and km/h, with what each speed and incline grade actually demands.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on manufacturer specifications and manuals, and independent sources.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#the-chart" class="btn-ghost">The Conversion Chart</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Treadmills in the US display miles per hour. Training plans are written in minutes per
          mile. Most running apps and anyone outside the US think in kilometres. Three units for
          one thing, and the arithmetic is the reason plenty of good sessions get run at the
          wrong intensity.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#the-maths">The Conversion, and How to Do It in Your Head</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-chart">Speed, Pace and km/h</a></li>
            <li><a href="#mph-to-min-per-km">mph to Minutes per Kilometre, With 5K and 10K Times</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-it-feels-like">The Only Calibration That Travels</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-equivalents">What an Incline Is Worth</a></li>
            <li><a href="#speed-and-incline">Speed and Incline Together: How Hard Each Combination Is</a></li>
            <li><a href="#one-percent">The 1% Incline Convention</a></li>
            <li><a href="#treadmill-vs-outdoor">Why Treadmill Pace and Road Pace Differ</a></li>
            <li><a href="#using-the-chart">Using This to Set Up a Session</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walking-pace">Walking Pace, Steps and Distance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walkers-vs-runners">What Treadmill Speeds Walkers and Runners Actually Use</a></li>
            <li><a href="#pace-by-goal">Target Paces by Goal</a></li>
            <li><a href="#race-goal-paces">Treadmill Speed for a 5K, 10K, Half or Marathon Goal Time</a></li>
            <li><a href="#metric-plans">If Your Plan Is in Kilometres</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Two Numbers to Remember</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="the-maths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Conversion, and How to Do It in Your Head</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Minutes per mile = 60 ÷ mph.</strong> That is the whole formula. At 6 mph you
            are covering a mile every 10 minutes; at 4 mph, every 15.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>km/h = mph × 1.61.</strong> Close enough to multiply by 1.6 mentally, which is
            accurate to within a rounding error at any treadmill speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two anchors worth memorising, because everything else can be estimated from them: <strong>6 mph is a 10-minute mile</strong> and <strong>4 mph is a 15-minute
            mile</strong>. From there, each 0.5 mph above 6 takes roughly 45 seconds off the mile,
            and the increments get smaller as speed rises — which is why the difference between
            9 and 9.5 mph feels smaller on the clock than the difference between 4 and 4.5.
          </p>
          <p>
            That non-linearity catches people out when following a plan. Adding 0.5 mph at
            walking pace changes your mile time by nearly two minutes; adding 0.5 mph at 9 mph
            changes it by about twenty seconds. The dial moves evenly and the effect does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Speed, Pace and km/h</h2>
<figure class="not-prose my-8">
            <img src="/charts/treadmill-pace-chart.svg" width="720" height="420" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full h-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-xl"
              alt="Line chart of treadmill speed against pace per mile: 3 mph is a 20:00 mile, 6 mph is 10:00, 7.5 mph is 8:00 and 10 mph is 6:00." />
            <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">Pace per mile against speed from 3 to 12 mph. Pace = 60 ÷ mph.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">mph</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Min / mile</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">km/h</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Typically</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">2.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">30:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3.2</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Slow walk, recovery</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">2.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">24:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Comfortable walk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">20:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4.8</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Standard walking pace</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">17:09</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.6</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Brisk walk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">4.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">15:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.4</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Fast walk / power walk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">4.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">13:20</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.2</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Walk-jog transition</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Easy jog</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10:55</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Steady jog</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Comfortable run</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:14</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Steady run</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">7.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:34</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Moderate effort</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">7.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Tempo for many</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:30</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12.9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Hard steady</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:04</td><td class="px-4 py-2">13.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Threshold / intervals</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">9.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:40</td><td class="px-4 py-2">14.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Interval pace</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">9.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:19</td><td class="px-4 py-2">15.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Fast intervals</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">16.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Short reps</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">11.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:27</td><td class="px-4 py-2">17.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Sprint reps</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">12.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">19.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Top speed on most machines</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            The "typically" column describes what a given speed usually represents for a
            reasonably fit adult, and it is a rough guide rather than a prescription. An easy jog
            for one person is a hard effort for another, and the effort labels that matter are the
            ones in the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mph-to-min-per-km" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>mph to Minutes per Kilometre, With 5K and 10K Times</h2>
<p>
            Most running apps, watches set to metric and nearly every race outside the US work in
            minutes per kilometre, while a US treadmill shows mph. <strong>Minutes per km = 60 ÷
            (mph × 1.609).</strong> At 6 mph that is 9.7 km/h and a 6:13 kilometre; at 7.5 mph it
            is 12.1 km/h and just under 5:00 per kilometre.
          </p>
          <p>
            The two right-hand columns show how long a 5K and a 10K take if you hold that speed
            the whole way, which answers the question most people are really asking when they
            look up a speed: how long will this take me?
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">mph</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">km/h</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Min / km</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">5K time</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">10K time</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4.8</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12:26</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:02:08</td><td class="px-4 py-2">2:04:16</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.6</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10:39</td><td class="px-4 py-2">53:16</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:46:31</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">4.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.4</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:19</td><td class="px-4 py-2">46:36</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:33:12</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">4.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.2</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:17</td><td class="px-4 py-2">41:25</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:22:51</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:27</td><td class="px-4 py-2">37:17</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:14:34</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:47</td><td class="px-4 py-2">33:54</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:07:47</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:13</td><td class="px-4 py-2">31:04</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:02:08</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.2</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:01</td><td class="px-4 py-2">30:04</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:00:08</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:44</td><td class="px-4 py-2">28:41</td><td class="px-4 py-2">57:21</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">7.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:20</td><td class="px-4 py-2">26:38</td><td class="px-4 py-2">53:16</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">7.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:58</td><td class="px-4 py-2">24:51</td><td class="px-4 py-2">49:43</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12.9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:40</td><td class="px-4 py-2">23:18</td><td class="px-4 py-2">46:36</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">13.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:23</td><td class="px-4 py-2">21:56</td><td class="px-4 py-2">43:52</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">9.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">14.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:09</td><td class="px-4 py-2">20:43</td><td class="px-4 py-2">41:25</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">9.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">15.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3:55</td><td class="px-4 py-2">19:37</td><td class="px-4 py-2">39:15</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">16.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3:44</td><td class="px-4 py-2">18:38</td><td class="px-4 py-2">37:17</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">11.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">17.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3:23</td><td class="px-4 py-2">16:57</td><td class="px-4 py-2">33:54</td></tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">12.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">19.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3:06</td><td class="px-4 py-2">15:32</td><td class="px-4 py-2">31:04</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Times are rounded to the nearest second and assume a steady speed from the first
            metre, so a real run with a warm-up and a slower first kilometre will come in a little
            longer. Two rows are worth remembering: <strong>6.2 mph is almost exactly 10 km/h and a
            6-minute kilometre</strong>, a 30-minute 5K and an hour-long 10K; and <strong>7.5 mph is
            a sub-5:00 kilometre</strong>, a 25-minute 5K. If your machine can switch its display to
            km/h, it is usually under a units setting in the maintenance or settings menu, but the
            belt and motor do not change; only the numbers on the screen do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-feels-like" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Only Calibration That Travels</h2>
<p>
            Numbers are useful for repeating a session; they are useless for judging whether the
            intensity is right for you today. For that, use breathing, which needs no calibration
            and does not flatter you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Easy.</strong> Full sentences without effort. You could keep going much
            longer than you plan to. Most of your training should be here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Steady.</strong> Short sentences. Comfortable but clearly working. This is
            where most people spend too much of their week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Threshold.</strong> A few words at a time. Sustainable for perhaps 20 to 40
            minutes and no longer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hard.</strong> Single words. Interval territory, sustainable for a few
            minutes at most.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same mph will sit in different rows on different days depending on sleep, heat,
            fatigue and how recently you ate. When the chart and your breathing disagree, believe
            your breathing. Our <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">training guide</a> covers how to distribute those intensities across a week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-equivalents" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What an Incline Is Worth</h2>
<p>
            Raising the grade increases cardiovascular demand without increasing speed, which is
            the treadmill's most useful trick. A rough working guide for walking:
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[460px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Grade</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">In degrees</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">3 mph walk feels roughly like</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">0%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">0.0&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">A 3 mph flat walk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">2%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1.1&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">A slightly brisker walk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">2.9&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">A fast 4 mph walk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4.6&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">A gentle jog</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.7&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Just short of a 5 mph jog</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">12%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.8&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Close to a 5 mph jog, without the impact</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">15%</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.5&deg;</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Genuinely demanding for most people</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            These are approximations rather than measured equivalences — actual energy cost varies
            with body weight, gait and fitness — but the shape is reliable: incline buys a large
            increase in effort for no increase in impact per footfall.
          </p>
          <p>
            Note how modest the angles are. Fifteen per cent is only 8.5 degrees, which is why a
            grade that looks trivial on a protractor is punishing underfoot. Our <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">grade conversion guide</a> covers that in full, and one caveat applies throughout: these figures assume you are
            not <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>holding the handrails</a>. Leaning transfers work to your arms and the numbers stop
            meaning anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-and-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Speed and Incline Together: How Hard Each Combination Is</h2>
<p>
            The incline table above is a rule of thumb. For a number, the standard tool is the
            pair of metabolic equations published by the American College of Sports Medicine in
            its <em>Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription</em>, which estimate oxygen
            cost from treadmill speed and grade. Converted to METs (multiples of resting energy
            use), they give the figures below.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Speed</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">0%</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">5%</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">10%</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">15%</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">2.0 mph walk</td><td class="px-4 py-2">2.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3.9</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.7</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.0 mph walk</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.4</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.4</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9.5</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.5 mph walk</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.5</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10.9</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5.0 mph run</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10.4</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">13.8</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.0 mph run</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10.2</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">14.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">16.4</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">7.0 mph run</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11.7</td><td class="px-4 py-2">14.1</td><td class="px-4 py-2">16.6</td><td class="px-4 py-2">19.0</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8.0 mph run</td><td class="px-4 py-2">13.3</td><td class="px-4 py-2">16.0</td><td class="px-4 py-2">18.8</td><td class="px-4 py-2">21.5</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Read across a row to see what grade does at a fixed speed, and down a column to see
            what speed does at a fixed grade. Three things stand out. First, a 3 mph walk at 10%
            (about 7.4 METs) sits close to a 5 mph jog on the flat (about 8.7), and at 15% it
            passes it, which is the arithmetic behind incline walking as a low-impact substitute
            for running. Second, grade costs a walker far more than a runner: the ACSM walking
            equation counts grade at twice the weight of the running equation, so 5% adds about 2
            METs to a 3 mph walk and about 1.7 to a 5 mph run despite the run being much faster.
            Third, everything here clears the 3-MET line that is conventionally used for moderate
            intensity except a 2 mph flat stroll.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. The walking equation is intended for roughly 1.9 to 3.7 mph and the
            running equation for 5 mph and up, so the 4 to 5 mph band, where people switch
            between walking and jogging, is not covered well by either. And the equations
            estimate an average person who is not holding the rails; your own cost will differ
            with body weight, stride and fitness. Our guide to <a href="/what-are-mets-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">METs on a treadmill</a> explains the unit, and <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk on</a> covers where to start on grade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-percent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The 1% Incline Convention</h2>
<p>
            The advice to set <a href="/treadmill-vs-running-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>1% incline</a> to "simulate outdoor running" comes from a 1996 study
            that found the energy cost of treadmill running matched outdoor running at around 1%
            grade — but specifically at faster paces, roughly 7 mph and above, where air
            resistance outdoors becomes a meaningful factor.
          </p>
          <p>
            At easier paces the difference is small enough to ignore. A walker or an easy jogger
            setting 1% is not doing anything wrong, and is not correcting for much either.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our view: 1% is a harmless default and a reasonable habit, particularly if you are
            training for outdoor races and want your treadmill paces to correspond to your road
            paces. It is not the meaningful variable it is often presented as, and it is not worth
            worrying about if your machine starts at 0%.
          </p>
          <p>
            What matters considerably more is the absence of wind, weather and terrain variation,
            none of which 1% addresses. Treadmill running is easier than road running in ways a
            single grade setting does not capture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-vs-outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why Treadmill Pace and Road Pace Differ</h2>
<p>
            Runners frequently find their treadmill pace does not match their outdoor pace, in
            either direction, and there are several reasons that have nothing to do with fitness.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No air resistance.</strong> Indoors you are not pushing through air, which at
            faster paces is a real saving — this is what the 1% convention addresses.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No terrain variation.</strong> A flat belt has no camber, no kerbs, no
            surface changes and no gradient unless you add one. Outdoor "flat" almost never is.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No heat dissipation.</strong> Running indoors without moving air means body
            heat builds faster, which makes a given pace feel harder — often the reason a
            treadmill session feels tougher than the equivalent road run.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console calibration drift.</strong> Belt speed is generally accurate but can
            drift with <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt wear</a> and tension. If your treadmill pace and your GPS watch disagree
            consistently, the machine may need attention — our <a href="/how-to-adjust-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a> covers the most common cause, and <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is my treadmill speed accurate?</a> shows how to check the belt against a stopwatch.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical takeaway: treat treadmill pace as its own scale. Compare treadmill
            sessions to treadmill sessions and road runs to road runs, rather than expecting them
            to be interchangeable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using-the-chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Using This to Set Up a Session</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Following a plan written in minutes per mile.</strong> Find your target pace
            in the chart and read across to mph. A plan asking for 9:14 miles is 6.5 mph. If it
            falls between rows, round down rather than up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Setting interval pace.</strong> Take a pace you could hold for 30 minutes and
            go 20 to 40 seconds per mile faster — in mph terms, roughly 0.3 to 0.6 faster. Set it
            for the final rep, not the first. Our <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval guide</a> covers the structures.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Setting easy pace.</strong> Take the pace you would naturally choose and go
            slower. Most people's "easy" is steady, which is the single most common training
            error.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Substituting incline for speed.</strong> If a plan calls for a hard effort and
            you would rather protect your joints, hold your usual pace and add grade using the
            incline table. Not identical, and close enough to be genuinely useful.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Walking Pace, Steps and Distance</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmill use happens between 2.5 and 4 mph, and walkers are usually thinking in
            steps or minutes rather than in pace. A few conversions make the chart more useful.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Steps per mile</strong> average roughly 2,000 for an adult walking, though it
            varies considerably with height — a shorter walker may take 2,300 and a taller one
            1,800. At 3 mph you are covering a mile every 20 minutes, so a 30-minute walk is about
            1.5 miles and roughly 3,000 steps. A 45-minute walk at that pace lands near 4,500.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reaching 10,000 steps</strong> on a treadmill alone would take around 100
            minutes at 3 mph. That is why the target is better treated as a daily total including
            ordinary movement rather than something to achieve in one session. A 30-minute
            treadmill walk contributing 3,000 to 3,500 of them is a substantial share of a day.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Raising pace versus raising time.</strong> For step count, time matters more
            than speed — walking faster covers more ground per step but does not add many steps
            per minute. If your goal is a step target, add minutes. If your goal is
            cardiovascular, add incline. Our <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill guide</a> covers the machines built for exactly this range, most of which are used far below
            their top speed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walkers-vs-runners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Treadmill Speeds Walkers and Runners Actually Use</h2>
<p>
            The chart runs from 2 to 12 mph, but almost nobody uses the whole range. Walkers live
            in the bottom third of it and most recreational runners in the middle, and knowing
            which band you belong in matters when you are choosing a machine as much as when you
            are setting a session.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Speed band</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Pace / mile</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Who typically uses it</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">1.0&ndash;2.0 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">60:00&ndash;30:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">First sessions, rehab, walking at a standing desk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">2.0&ndash;3.0 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">30:00&ndash;20:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Everyday walking, many older walkers, walking pads</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">3.0&ndash;4.0 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">20:00&ndash;15:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Brisk fitness walking, incline walking</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">4.0&ndash;5.0 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">15:00&ndash;12:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Power walking or a slow jog: the walk-run switch</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5.0&ndash;6.5 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">12:00&ndash;9:14</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Easy running for most recreational runners</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">6.5&ndash;8.5 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:14&ndash;7:04</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Steady and tempo running for regular runners</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">8.5&ndash;12.0 mph</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:04&ndash;5:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Intervals, strides and sprints</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>The walk-run switch.</strong> Somewhere around 4 to 5 mph, usually near 4.5,
            walking starts to feel more awkward than jogging and most adults break into a run on
            their own. Taller people with longer strides tend to switch later, shorter people
            earlier. Fast walking just below that point is surprisingly hard work, which is why
            power walkers at 4.3 mph often find a 4.6 mph jog feels easier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What this means for the machine.</strong> If everything you do sits under
            4 mph, a walking treadmill or a walking pad covers it, and its top speed is not a
            limitation. If you jog even occasionally, look at the running bands: easy running
            needs 5 to 6.5 mph, and intervals need 8 mph and up with a belt long enough to stride
            out. Whether a pad can stretch that far is covered in <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">can you run on a walking pad</a>,
            and the walking end of the chart is broken down by goal, fitness level and age in <a href="/what-speed-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what speed to walk on a treadmill</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pace-by-goal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Target Paces by Goal</h2>
<p>
            A rough orientation for where to sit on the chart depending on what you are training
            for. These are starting points to adjust by feel, not prescriptions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>General health and fitness.</strong> Most of your time between 3 and 3.5 mph
            with some incline, or 5 to 6 mph if you run. The intensity that matters is
            conversational, and the speed that produces it will change as you get fitter — which
            is the point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Building an aerobic base.</strong> Slower than feels satisfying. If you can
            hold full sentences you are in the right place. For most runners this is 5 to 6 mph
            even if they can run considerably faster, and resisting the urge to push is most of
            the skill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Training toward a 5K.</strong> Easy running at 5 to 6 mph for most sessions,
            with intervals around 7 to 8 mph once a week. The race itself is likely to be run
            somewhere between those, and the mistake is training at race pace all the time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Training toward a 10K or longer.</strong> The same easy range for the bulk of
            the work, one longer session, and threshold efforts around 7 to 7.5 mph rather than
            short fast intervals. Endurance is built by time on feet at a manageable intensity.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight management.</strong> Duration and consistency beat intensity here, and
            incline beats speed. Longer sessions at a pace you can sustain and repeat several
            times a week do more than short hard ones you dread. Our <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to that</a> covers the reasoning, including why the console's calorie figure is not the number to
            optimise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Returning from a break or an injury.</strong> Start below where you think you
            should be and add time before pace. The chart is useful here mainly as a record —
            knowing you walked 30 minutes at 3.2 mph this week makes next week's small increase
            deliberate rather than accidental.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="race-goal-paces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill Speed for a 5K, 10K, Half or Marathon Goal Time</h2>
<p>
            Working backwards from a finish time is the most common reason runners look up this
            chart. Divide the goal time by the race distance in miles to get the pace, then 60 by
            the pace to get mph. The table does that for the goal times people search for most.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
            <table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white text-left">
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Race</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Goal time</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Pace / mile</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Pace / km</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Exact mph</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-3 font-black">Set the belt to</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">35:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11:16</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.33</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.4</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">30:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:39</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.21</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.3</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">25:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:03</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.46</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.5</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">5K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">20:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:26</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9.32</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9.4</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:10:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11:16</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.33</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.4</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:00:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:39</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.21</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.3</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">50:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:03</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.46</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.5</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">10K</td><td class="px-4 py-2">45:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:15</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:30</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.28</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8.3</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Half marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">2:30:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11:27</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:07</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.24</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.3</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Half marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">2:00:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:09</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:41</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.55</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.6</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Half marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">1:45:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:01</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:59</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.49</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.5</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:00:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">11:27</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7:07</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.24</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.3</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:30:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">10:18</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6:24</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.83</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5.9</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:00:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">9:09</td><td class="px-4 py-2">5:41</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.55</td><td class="px-4 py-2">6.6</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-bold">Marathon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">3:30:00</td><td class="px-4 py-2">8:01</td><td class="px-4 py-2">4:59</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.49</td><td class="px-4 py-2">7.5</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Why the last column rounds up.</strong> Treadmills move in 0.1 mph steps, and
            the exact speed for most goals falls between two of them. A 30-minute 5K needs 6.21
            mph; at 6.2 you finish in about 30:04, so the belt has to go to 6.3 (about 29:35) to get
            under. For a goal race, set the next step up. For everyday training at a pace, the
            earlier advice still holds: round down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not train at these speeds every day.</strong> Goal pace is for a portion
            of one or two sessions a week; most running should sit slower, in the easy band for
            your level. A half-marathon or marathon goal is also less about whether you can hold
            the speed for ten minutes than whether you can hold it for two to five hours, which
            is built by long runs well below it. Our <a href="/couch-to-5k-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">couch to 5K treadmill plan</a> and <a href="/treadmill-marathon-training/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill marathon training guide</a> show how goal pace fits into a full week. If you are racing outdoors, many runners add
            the 1% grade covered above to these settings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="metric-plans" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If Your Plan Is in Kilometres</h2>
<p>
            Plenty of training plans, apps and race distances are metric while US treadmills are
            not, which adds a second conversion. The arithmetic is the same shape.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Minutes per kilometre = 60 ÷ km/h</strong>, and km/h is mph × 1.6. So 6 mph is
            9.7 km/h, which is about 6:13 per kilometre. A useful anchor: <strong>10 km/h — about 6.2 mph — is a 6-minute kilometre</strong>, which is the
            metric equivalent of the <a href="/what-speed-is-a-10-minute-mile-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>10-minute mile</a> as a mental reference point.
          </p>
          <p>
            The other conversion worth having is distance. A 5K is 3.11 miles and a 10K is 6.21
            miles, so a treadmill reporting miles will show 3.1 and 6.2 respectively. A half
            marathon is 13.1 miles and a marathon 26.2. If your machine displays only miles and
            your goal is metric, those four numbers cover almost every case.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where this matters most is interval sessions written as distances — "6 × 400m", for
            instance. Four hundred metres is a quarter mile, so on a mile-reporting treadmill you
            are looking for 0.25 on the distance readout, and at 8 mph that takes about 1 minute
            52 seconds. Working in time rather than distance is usually easier on a treadmill, and
            converting the session once in advance saves doing arithmetic mid-effort.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Two Numbers to Remember</h2>
<p>
            <strong>6 mph is a 10-minute mile. 4 mph is a 15-minute mile.</strong> Everything else
            can be estimated from those two, and <strong>mph × 1.6 gives km/h</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use the chart to translate a plan into machine settings, and use your breathing to
            decide whether the setting is right today. When they disagree, breathing wins —
            the chart does not know you slept badly.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you want a session to be harder, reach for the incline column rather than the
            speed column. Same cardiovascular gain, considerably less impact.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Reading Treadmill Speeds</h2>
          <p>Three of these come from treating a number on a console as though it meant the same thing every day.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming 0.5 mph means the same thing at every speed</h3>
              <p>The dial moves evenly and the effect does not. Adding 0.5 mph at walking pace changes your mile time by nearly two minutes; adding it at 9 mph changes it by about twenty seconds. Plans written in pace do not increment the way a speed dial does, which is why converting matters.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Running every session by the number rather than by feel</h3>
              <p>The same mph sits at a different effort on different days depending on sleep, heat, fatigue and food. Use the chart to translate a plan into settings and your breathing to judge whether it is right today. Full sentences means easy; single words means hard. When they disagree, believe your breathing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Holding the rails while using the incline equivalents</h3>
              <p>Every incline comparison on this page assumes your hands are off the handrails, or resting lightly at most. Leaning transfers work to your arms and the frame while the console keeps reporting the same grade, so the equivalence stops holding. If you cannot walk the grade without gripping, lower it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting treadmill and road paces to match</h3>
              <p>No air resistance, no terrain variation and poorer heat dissipation mean the two are genuinely different. Compare treadmill sessions to treadmill sessions and road runs to road runs. A 1% grade addresses part of the difference at faster paces and does not make them interchangeable.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">6 mph is a 10-minute mile, 4 mph is a 15-minute mile, and
            mph × 1.6 gives km/h.</strong> Everything else follows from those three.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use the chart to translate a plan into machine settings, and your breathing to judge
            whether it suits you today. When you want a session harder, reach for the <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">incline column</a> rather than the speed column.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
