        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How do I convert treadmill mph to minutes per mile?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Divide 60 by the speed in mph. At 6 mph that is a 10-minute mile; at 4 mph, a 15-minute mile. Those two are worth memorising because everything else can be estimated from them. To get km/h, multiply mph by 1.61 — or just 1.6, which is accurate enough at any treadmill speed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed is a 10-minute mile on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              6.0 mph, which is 9.7 km/h. A 9-minute mile is about 6.7 mph, an 8-minute mile is 7.5 mph, and a 12-minute mile is 5.0 mph. If your target pace falls between the speeds your machine offers, round down rather than up.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What incline equals running on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Roughly, walking at 3 mph on a 10 to 12% grade is comparable in energy cost to jogging at about 5 mph on the flat (around 7.4 to 8.3 METs against 8.7 by the ACSM equations), at a fraction of the impact per footfall. These are approximations that vary with body weight and fitness, and they assume you are not holding the handrails.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I set the treadmill to 1% incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is a harmless default and a reasonable habit, particularly if you race outdoors. The 1996 study behind it found the energy cost matched outdoor running at 1% specifically at faster paces, around 7 mph and up. At walking and easy jogging paces the difference is small enough to ignore.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is my treadmill pace different from my outdoor pace?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Several reasons unrelated to fitness: no air resistance to push through, no camber or terrain variation, and poorer heat dissipation indoors, which makes a given pace feel harder. Console calibration can also drift with belt wear. Treat treadmill pace as its own scale rather than expecting the two to match.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What treadmill speed do I need for a 30-minute 5K?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A 30-minute 5K is a 9:39 mile, which is 6.21 mph or a 6:00 kilometre. Treadmills step in 0.1 mph, and 6.2 mph finishes in about 30:04, so set 6.3 mph to get under 30 minutes. A 25-minute 5K needs 7.5 mph and a 20-minute 5K needs 9.4 mph.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a good treadmill speed for walking and for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most people walk at 2.5 to 4 mph, with 3 to 3.5 mph a typical brisk pace (a 20:00 to 17:09 mile). Running usually starts around 4.5 to 5 mph, and most recreational runners do their easy miles between 5 and 6.5 mph. Faster than about 8.5 mph is interval and sprint territory for most people.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
